---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Prose from "../../components/Prose.astro";
import GuideData from "../../components/GuideData.astro";
import EveryCut from "../../components/EveryCut.astro";
import { bySlug, kicker } from "../../dossiers/registry.js";
import { dateStamp } from "../../dossiers/display.js";
const D = bySlug["wpi"];
import { period } from "../../dossiers/periods.js";
// wave 2 (lb/prices): the retired bases and the wholesale-to-retail plate
const PW93 = period("wpi-1993"), PW22 = period("wpi-2022"), PW11 = period("wpi");
---
<Base
  title="Wholesale Prices · India Atlas"
  description={D.description}
  nav="guides"
  dateStamp={dateStamp(D)}
  crumbLeft={`<a href="/guides/">Guides</a> / ${D.section} / <b>${D.crumbTitle}</b>`}
  crumbRight={`${D.statusNote}, <b>${D.version}</b>`}
  canonical="https://atlas.thisindianlife.today/guides/wpi/"
  ogImage={`/og/${D.slug}.png`}
>
<div class="page">

  <div class="titleblock break">
    <div class="kicker">{kicker(D)}</div>
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <h1 set:html={D.headline} />
      <p class="lede">Before anything reaches a shop shelf, it is sold once already: mill to trader, refinery to pump, loom to garment exporter. This is the price of that first sale, and it moves to a different music than the one you pay.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="meta">
      <span>Source <b>OEA, DPIIT</b></span>
      <span>Frequency <b>Monthly</b></span>
      <span>Base year <b>2022–23 = 100</b> (2011–12 to April 2026)</span>
      <span>Coverage <b>All-India, goods only</b></span>
      <span>Latest print <b id="m-latest">–</b></span>
      <span>Released <b id="m-released">–</b></span>
    </div>
    <div class="numbers">
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Headline WPI, y/y</div><div class="v acc" id="n-head">–</div><div class="d" id="d-head">–</div><div class="nspark" id="nspark-head"></div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Food index, y/y</div><div class="v" id="n-food">–</div><div class="d" id="d-food">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Fuel &amp; power, y/y</div><div class="v" id="n-fuel">–</div><div class="d" id="d-fuel">–</div></div>
      <div class="num"><div class="l">Manufactured, y/y</div><div class="v" id="n-mfg">–</div><div class="d" id="d-mfg">–</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- § 01 current reading -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">01</span><h2>The current reading</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit" id="rd-unit">–</span></div>
    <div class="reading-top">
      <div><span class="rk">Current reading</span><div class="big" id="rb-big">–</div></div>
      <p class="rt-cap" id="rt-cap">–</p>
      <div class="rt-side"><span class="rk">One month earlier</span><div class="rt-side-v" id="rb-prev">–</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="reading-cols">
      <div class="reading-col">
        <span class="mk">What the print says</span>
        <Prose slot="wpi.current-reading.what-the-print-says" />
      </div>
      <div class="reading-col now">
        <span class="mk">What it does not say</span>
        <Prose slot="wpi.current-reading.what-it-does-not-say" />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 02 the new base -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">02</span><h2>The new base: two rulers through the overlap</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, base 2022–23 and base 2011–12</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-nb" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3 id="nb-title">–</h3>
            <div class="sub" id="nb-sub">–</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-nb">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-nb">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-nb">
            <button class="chip" data-key="nb_new"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Base 2022–23, current</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="nb_old"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Base 2011–12, ended April 2026</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-nb"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: <a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT</a>, via MoSPI eSankhyiki, both lines are the published headline index, year-on-year computed from index levels within each base, the two are never joined into one series</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The Office of the Economic Adviser has moved the index to a new base year, 2022–23. The new basket is larger, 953 items against 697, and it is weighted to the economy of 2022–23 rather than 2011–12. Crude petroleum and natural gas has also moved, from primary articles into fuel and power, so the parts of the two indices are not like for like.</p>
            <p>The blue line is the new index; the grey line is the old one, which the OEA stopped after April 2026. Each year-on-year figure is computed inside its own base, from that base's own levels. Where both lines exist, they read the same months with two different rulers. The gap between them is the new basket at work, not an error, and it is why we never join the two into one number.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of September 2026</span>
            <p>On the new base, wholesale inflation was <b id="nb-new">–</b> in <span id="nb-new-m">–</span>. In <span id="nb-both-m">–</span>, the last month both indices describe, the new base read <b id="nb-both-new">–</b> and the old base <b id="nb-both-old">–</b>. Across the <span id="nb-n">–</span> months the two share, they part by at most <b id="nb-gap">–</b>, in <span id="nb-gap-m">–</span>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:88px">
      <figure id="cb-long" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3 id="long-title">Thirty years of wholesale prices, on four rulers</h3>
            <div class="sub">Headline WPI, year-on-year, per cent, {PW93.firstShort} to {PW22.short}: bases 1993–94, 2004–05, 2011–12 and 2022–23, each computed inside its own base, drawn side by side where they overlap, never joined</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-long">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-long">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-long">
            <button class="chip" data-key="b1993"><i style="background:#C9CED6"></i>Base 1993–94</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="b2004"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Base 2004–05</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="b2011"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Base 2011–12</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="b2022"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Base 2022–23</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-long"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT, via MoSPI eSankhyiki, the published headline index on each base, year-on-year from its own levels; the 2011–12 line starts in 2016, where this guide's bundle starts</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The wholesale index has been rebuilt three times since 1994, each time with a bigger basket weighted to a newer economy: 437 items on the 1993–94 base, 676 on 2004–05, 697 on 2011–12 and 953 on 2022–23. Each colour is one basket. Where two colours run over the same months, they are two rulers measuring the same economy, and their small disagreement is the difference between the baskets.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {PW22.stamp}</span>
            <p>The highest reading in three decades is <b id="long-peak">–</b>; the lowest is <b id="long-trough">–</b>. Today's reading on the newest ruler is <b id="long-now">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 03 register -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">03</span><h2>The register of series</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Weights, latest, year ago, base 2011–12, to April 2026</span></div>
    <div class="break ledger">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Series</th><th>Weight</th><th>Latest y/y</th><th>vs prev. month</th><th>Year ago</th><th class="spark">2016 to 2026</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody id="ledger-body"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 04 composition -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">04</span><h2>What is in the basket</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Weights, per cent, base 2011–12</span></div>
    <div class="break basket">
      <div class="bar" id="basket-bar"></div>
      <div class="labels" id="basket-labels"></div>
      <p class="note">Three groups, and the skew is the whole story: nearly two-thirds of the wholesale basket is manufactured goods, which barely move, while the volatile third, what comes out of the ground and what burns, supplies nearly all of the drama. The food index, published separately, straddles primary articles and manufactured food.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 05 plate 1 -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">05</span><h2>Plate 1: the headline and its parts</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, monthly, base 2011–12, to April 2026</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-main" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Ten years at the factory gate: the calm core, the volatile rim</h3>
            <div class="sub">WPI headline and components, year-on-year, per cent, Jan 2016 to Apr 2026</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-main">–</div><div class="c">Apr 2026, headline y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-main">
            <button class="chip" data-key="headline"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Headline</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="primary"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Primary articles</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="manufactured"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Manufactured</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="food_index"><i style="background:#878D99"></i>Food index</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-main"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: <a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Office of the Economic Adviser, DPIIT</a>, release of 14 May 2026, headline computed from the three major groups via official weights, food index published directly, fuel &amp; power shown on its own plate: it would own this one's axis</span>
            <span class="dl">
              <button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span>
              <a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a>
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Watch the grey line that barely ripples: that is two-thirds of the economy's goods. Then watch the dark one, primary articles, which swings through double digits in both directions. The blue headline is their weighted truce: it always ends up nearer the calm line than the wild one, which is precisely what the weights mean.</p>
            <p>Notice 2023: the headline spent seven months below zero while consumer inflation ran above five. Nothing was broken. Wholesale goods were retracing the commodity spike while retail services kept climbing: the two indices were measuring different terrain and both were right.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of May 2026</span>
            <p>The headline has climbed for ten straight months: from <b id="ex-hsep">–</b> last September to <b id="ex-hapr">–</b> in April, a reading last exceeded in October 2022, when the post-invasion commodity shock was still working through the pipeline.</p>
            <p>The shape of the climb changed in April. Until March it was a broad, gentle firming; April is a fuel event stacked on top of it. Whether it persists depends on one line: the next plate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 06 the measure -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">06</span><h2>What this measures</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="wm-grid">
      <div>
        <p>The Wholesale Price Index tracks the first transaction in the economy: the price a miner gets for ore, a mill for flour, a refinery for diesel, a power station for a unit of electricity. When we say wholesale inflation is 8.4 per cent, we mean this basket of first sales costs <b>8.4 per cent more than it did a year ago</b>.</p>
        <p>Two absences define it. There are no services here: and services are more than half of the economy you live in. And there are no retail margins: the price is quoted at the factory gate, before transport, before the distributor's cut, before the shop. It is a producer's-eye view of inflation, not a consumer's.</p>
        <p>It is also a Laspeyres index on a 2011–12 basket, older even than the CPI's. The economy has since grown entire industries the basket has never heard of. Treat the level with respect and the composition with suspicion.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="wm-rail">
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, i</span>
          <b>WPI is not a preview of CPI.</b> Different baskets, different weights, different taxes. The pass-through is real but slow, partial, and easy to over-read in a single month.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, ii</span>
          <b>Provisional means provisional.</b> The first print is revised to final about eight weeks later. The headline you read today is a draft.
        </aside>
        <aside class="margin-note">
          <span class="mk">Margin note, iii</span>
          <b>Computed, cross-checked.</b> Our headline is built from the three major-group indices via official weights; it tracks the published figure within 0.1 pt across the decade.
        </aside>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 07 component plates -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">07</span><h2>The parts, one plate each</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">y/y %, monthly, base 2011–12, to April 2026</span></div>

    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-fuel" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Fuel &amp; power: an eighth of the basket, all of the weather</h3>
            <div class="sub">Weight 13.15, year-on-year, per cent, 2016–2026</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-fuel">–</div><div class="c">Apr 2026, y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-fuel"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: OEA, DPIIT, <span id="pf-fuel">–</span></span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is the most violent line in Indian price statistics: a 51 per cent peak in June 2022, a 23 per cent trough in the same cycle, and everything in between. It answers to global crude, to administered electricity tariffs, and to the arithmetic of the year-ago denominator, usually in that order of volume.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of May 2026</span>
            <p>April's <b id="ex-fuel">–</b> is the highest print since the 2022 shock, arrived in a single step from near zero. What sits behind the step is not disclosed in this table, the index records the price, not the cause, and we will not invent one. What the table does say: the last time this line moved like this, the headline followed for a year.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:104px">
      <figure id="cb-mfg" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Manufactured products: the two-thirds that hardly moves</h3>
            <div class="sub">Weight 64.23, year-on-year, per cent, 2016–2026</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-mfg">–</div><div class="c">Apr 2026, y/y</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-mfg"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: OEA, DPIIT, <span id="pf-mfg">–</span></span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>If the fuel line is the weather, this is the climate: metals, chemicals, textiles, machinery: the processed bulk of the goods economy. It lives in a narrow band, and when it leaves the band, as in 2021–22, the cause is imported: input costs arriving from the two volatile groups with a lag of a few months.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of May 2026</span>
            <p>At <b id="ex-mfg">–</b>, manufactured inflation is at a three-year high but still inside its historical murmur. The question for the second half of 2026 is whether April's fuel shock passes through into this line. That pass-through, if it comes, is the one that reaches consumer prices.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 08 the 29 groups -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>The groups, one by one</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">29 groups, y/y %, base 2011–12, 2016 to April 2026</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-groups" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>Twenty-nine groups: where the average stops being useful</h3>
            <div class="sub">All 2-digit groups, year-on-year, per cent, Jan 2016 to Apr 2026</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-groups">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-groups">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="gclass" id="cap-wild">–</div>
          <div class="ggrid" id="grid-wild"></div>
          <div class="gclass" id="cap-calm">–</div>
          <div class="ggrid" id="grid-calm"></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src"><span>Source: OEA, DPIIT, panels ranked by latest y/y within class, two shared axes by volatility class, disclosed above each grid, blue where the latest y/y sits outside ±6% (<span id="pf-nhot">–</span> of 29 this print), every y/y computed from index levels</span></span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv">CSV, all 29</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Each panel is one group on its own clock, drawn to an axis it shares with its class. Stop reading lines and start reading rows: when wholesale inflation moves, the commodity rim, the panels on the wide axis, does the moving, and the manufactured mass below barely registers. A flat panel is not a failed chart. It is the finding.</p>
            <p>The two axes are a concession, not a trick. Crude petroleum has swung through 130 points of y/y in a decade; beverages through four. One shared scale would iron twenty-two panels flat and tell you nothing. So the split is disclosed, and each class stays comparable within itself.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of May 2026</span>
            <p>April's fuel event is legible at group level: crude petroleum &amp; natural gas at <b id="ex-gcrude">–</b>, mineral oils at <b id="ex-goil">–</b>. Beside them sits other manufacturing at <b id="ex-gother">–</b>: that group is mostly jewellery, which is to say gold, not a factory story. The rim is hot almost end to end.</p>
            <p>The mass is quieter but not still. Textiles at <b id="ex-gtext">–</b> is the one calm-axis panel past the blue threshold, and basic metals, up on the wide axis at <b id="ex-gmet">–</b>, is running well ahead of the manufactured average. If April's fuel shock passes through, these are the panels to watch.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 the long tail: 134 sub-groups -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>The long tail: 134 sub-groups</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Extremes, y/y %, base 2011–12, full CSV</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-tail" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The extremes ledger: fifteen hottest, fifteen coldest</h3>
            <div class="sub">134 sub-groups, the deepest aggregate level the OEA publishes, latest y/y, Apr 2026</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-tail">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-tail">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="ledger tail-ledger">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Sub-group</th><th>Latest y/y</th><th>vs prev. month</th><th>Year ago</th><th class="spark">2016 to 2026</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody id="tail-body"></tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src"><span>Source: OEA, DPIIT, ranked by April 2026 y/y, <span id="pf-tail">–</span>, every y/y computed from index levels, CSV carries all 134 series, Jan 2016 to Apr 2026</span></span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv">CSV, all 134</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is where "inflation" stops being one number. The wholesale index is an average of averages, and every sub-group here is somebody's entire cost sheet: the rope-maker does not experience the headline, he experiences rope. In any given month the spread across these 134 series runs to tens of points; the headline sits somewhere in the middle and describes almost no one exactly.</p>
            <p>Thirty rows are shown; the field is 134. The rows in between are quieter, which is why they live in the CSV rather than on the page: a table of everything is a table of nothing.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of May 2026</span>
            <p>The April spread runs <b id="ex-tspan">–</b> points end to end: <span id="ex-ttopn">–</span> at <b id="ex-ttop">–</b> down to <span id="ex-tbotn">–</span> at <b id="ex-tbot">–</b>. Gold, fibres and wires carry the top of the table; the bottom is food processing and electronics.</p>
            <p>The median sub-group sits at <b id="ex-tmed">–</b>, and <b id="ex-tneg">–</b> of the 134 are below zero in the same month the headline runs past eight. That is what a fuel-led print looks like from underneath: narrow heat, wide calm, and pockets of outright deflation at the factory gate.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 10 the 697 items -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>The 697 items</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Extremes, y/y %, base 2011–12, full CSV</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-items" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3>The finest grain: fifteen hottest, fifteen coldest of 697</h3>
            <div class="sub">Every named item in the 2011-12 basket, with its lineage, latest y/y, Apr 2026</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-items">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-items">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="ledger tail-ledger">
            <table>
              <thead>
                <tr><th>Item</th><th>Latest y/y</th><th>vs prev. month</th><th>Year ago</th><th class="spark">2016 to 2026</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody id="items-body"></tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src"><span>Source: OEA, DPIIT, ranked by April 2026 y/y, <span id="pf-items">–</span>, every y/y computed from index levels, CSV carries the full cross-section: index level and y/y for every item, with lineage</span></span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv">CSV, all 697</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>This is the finest grain wholesale prices are published at: the level where "inflation" stops being a number and becomes onions, HR coils and jet fuel. Everything above this table is an average of something; these are the goods themselves, 697 of them, each with its own index and its own weather. And the deeper you go, the wider the field: sub-groups spread across tens of points in a month, items across hundreds.</p>
            <p>The lineage under each name is the path back up the tree, so a hot row can be traced to the aggregate it feeds. Thirty rows are shown; the rest are quieter, and they ship complete in the CSV.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of May 2026</span>
            <p>The April spread runs <b id="ex-ispan">–</b> points end to end: <span id="ex-itopn">–</span> at <b id="ex-itop">–</b> down to <span id="ex-ibotn">–</span> at <b id="ex-ibot">–</b>. The fuel event is legible item by item: petrol at <b id="ex-ipetrol">–</b>, ATF at <b id="ex-iatf">–</b>, HSD at <b id="ex-ihsd">–</b>: one refinery gate, three grades of the same shock.</p>
            <p>The median item sits at <b id="ex-imed">–</b>, and <b id="ex-ineg">–</b> of the 697 are below zero in the same month the headline runs past eight. Onion, the old headline-maker, prints <b id="ex-ionion">–</b>: this is not a food print, and the item table is where that is easiest to see.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 wholesale to retail (wave 2, lb/prices) -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>From the factory gate to the shop</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">WPI food index against CPI food, y/y %, base 2011–12 and 2012</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-w2r" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3 id="w2r-title">Wholesale food and retail food</h3>
            <div class="sub">WPI food index (base 2011–12) and CPI food and beverages (base 2012), year-on-year, per cent, <span id="w2r-span">–</span>: two different instruments from two different offices, drawn on one axis, never combined</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v" id="pv-w2r">–</div><div class="c" id="pc-w2r">–</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend" id="legend-w2r">
            <button class="chip" data-key="wpi"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Wholesale food (WPI)</button>
            <button class="chip" data-key="cpi"><i style="background:#C47A16"></i>Retail food (CPI)</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone" id="plot-w2r"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: OEA, DPIIT (WPI food index) and MoSPI, NSO (CPI food and beverages), via eSankhyiki, the lag is the shift of the retail line, in months, that best matches the wholesale line (highest correlation of the two y/y series over the shared months, shifts of 0 to 6 tried)</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><span class="sep"></span><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><span class="sep"></span><a href="https://eaindustry.nic.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source ↗</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>A tomato is sold at least twice: once in the mandi, once at the cart. The wholesale index records the first sale, the consumer index the second. The two baskets differ (the WPI food index has no restaurant meals; the CPI has no sugar sold to factories), and the retail price adds transport, rent and the shopkeeper's margin, which move slowly. So the retail line tracks the wholesale one without matching it point for point; the plate measures how closely, and with what delay.</p>
            <p>This is the link people mean when they say wholesale prices "pass through" to the shop. It is real for food. For manufactured goods the pass-through is weaker and slower, which is why a fall in the headline WPI rarely shows up as cheaper goods on the shelf.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of January 2026</span>
            <p>Over the <b id="w2r-n">–</b> months both series cover, the retail line matches the wholesale line best when shifted <b id="w2r-lag">–</b> later (correlation <b id="w2r-r">–</b>). Wholesale food swung between <b id="w2r-wrange">–</b>; retail food between <b id="w2r-crange">–</b>. Month to month, the wholesale line moved <b id="w2r-sd">–</b> the retail one.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 12 misread -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">12</span><h2>How to misread this number</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li>
        <span class="no">i.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Wholesale inflation is 8%, so consumer inflation will be 8%."</h3>
          <p>No. The wholesale basket contains no services and no retail margins: it cannot map one-to-one onto the cost of living. In 2023 the WPI spent seven months in deflation while the CPI ran above five per cent. The two indices were not contradicting each other; they were describing different parts of the economy, both correctly.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">ii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The headline is the whole story."</h3>
          <p>A single fuel month can add four points to the headline while two-thirds of the basket quietly does nothing. Before drawing any conclusion, open the groups. The composition of a print matters more than its size.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iii.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"Deflation at the gate means deflation in the shop."</h3>
          <p>Falling wholesale prices mean producers are paying less for inputs: relief that reaches consumers partially, slowly, or not at all, depending on who owns the margin in between. The 2023 wholesale deflation was invisible on most shop shelves.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">iv.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"The first print is the print."</h3>
          <p>WPI is released provisional and revised to final about eight weeks later. Revisions are usually decimal dust, but they move the levels: and because every y/y is a ratio of levels, a revised denominator moves last year's growth too. The vintage matters.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <span class="no">v.</span>
        <div>
          <h3>"It tells you what industry earns."</h3>
          <p>These are output prices at the gate, not profits. Costs, wages, credit, logistics, live outside the index entirely. A rising WPI is compatible with shrinking margins, and frequently coincides with them.</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

